  "title": "Erdős Problem #43",
  "statement": {
    "representation": "markdown-latex",
    "text": "If $A,B\\subset \\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$ are two Sidon sets such that $(A-A)\\cap(B-B)=\\{0\\}$ then is it true that $  \\binom{\\lvert A\\rvert}{2}+\\binom{\\lvert B\\rvert}{2}\\leq\\binom{f(N)}{2}+O(1), $ where $f(N)$ is the maximum possible size of a Sidon set in $\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$? If $\\lvert A\\rvert=\\lvert B\\rvert$ then can this bound be improved to $ \\binom{\\lvert A\\rvert}{2}+\\binom{\\lvert B\\rvert}{2}\\leq (1-c+o(1))\\binom{f(N)}{2} $ for some constant $c>0$?",
    "sha256": "5b64090f32e3da5f71d83335cdd10dc5fbe182eabd09d8029ceb15ff6cb083d4",
